<BaseLayout title="Editorial method" description="How Vision for Bharat develops, tests, sources, and revises its proposals.">
  <header class="page-intro">
    <div class="shell"><p class="kicker">Editorial method</p><h1>How an idea earns its place here.</h1><p class="lead">Good intentions are not enough. Proposals should expose their evidence, mechanism, costs, uncertainty, and strongest objections.</p></div>
  </header>
  <div class="prose-page shell narrow">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>1. Name the problem precisely</h2>
      <p>Begin with the people and systems affected. Separate a measurable condition from an interpretation of its causes. Avoid turning an anecdote into a national claim.</p>

      <h2>2. Label what we know</h2>
      <p>Facts, estimates, inferences, and values are different kinds of statements. Sources should be linked close to the claims they support, with a preference for legislation, official data, court decisions, audit reports, and original research.</p>

      <h2>3. Explain the mechanism</h2>
      <p>A proposal should say who acts, under which authority, in what sequence, at whose cost, and with what measure of success. If implementation depends on unresolved constitutional, fiscal, or federal questions, those belong in the centre of the essay.</p>

      <h2>4. Present the strongest objection</h2>
      <p>Trade-offs are not footnotes. Each developed proposal should address incentives, distributional effects, administrative capacity, misuse, opportunity cost, and plausible unintended consequences.</p>

      <h2>5. Make uncertainty useful</h2>
      <p>Open questions should identify what research, pilot, consultation, or new evidence would change the proposal. “We do not yet know” can be an actionable conclusion.</p>

      <h2>6. Revise in public</h2>
      <p>Material changes receive an updated date and a plain-language note. Corrections should be visible. Quiet copy-edits do not need ceremony, but changes to evidence, conclusions, or implementation do.</p>

      <h2>Editorial status</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seed idea:</strong> a direction worth recording, with substantial research still needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Working draft:</strong> a structured proposal whose mechanism and objections are being developed.</li>
        <li><strong>Developed proposal:</strong> a sourced argument with implementation paths and trade-offs.</li>
        <li><strong>Revised proposal:</strong> a developed proposal materially changed in response to evidence or critique.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
